@babyblueviper1  clean handoff and thank you for recomputing rather than reading, that’s twice now you’ve taken it at the recompute, which is exactly the discipline this whole thing is supposed to embody. greenlit, home’s set: hack-ens-recovery @ 7ddf465, your repo stays the authored type-1 reference, Damon’s the type-2.

what we actually have now is the full adversarial-a closure in one contest(): coordinate half (verifyAbsence + verifyScopeComplete) + value half (verifyValueFidelity), three people’s legs composing behind one flow, each independently recomputable. that’s the IScopeContestation reference done, absence, completeness, value-fidelity, all duals of the same bind / recompute shape.

@DamonZwicker  no rush at all, go through it whenever. and same to you, this was the good kind of build: four hands, no merge friction, every leg recomputed not trusted. grateful too. 🤝

next is just prose: I’ll take a first pass at folding the two settled points into the normative text, meaning forced to actual-resolution (recomputability, not a mode toggle), and type-1/type-2 as provenance-only with the 8281-commit + zkTLS-authenticate decomposition, when I’m back or when i get a bit of time. shout if anyone wants to grab it before then. 🍻

@GobRoss grabbing the prose pass on point one, the meaning axis. it's the recomputability leg so it's mine to write. I'll fold point two into the same draft and keep @DamonZwicker ‘s decomposition intact: type-1/type-2 as provenance-only, type-2's source-authenticated inputHash as 8281-commit then zkTLS-authenticate, two primitives composed, value-fidelity covering the commitment side and source-auth the orthogonal zkTLS leg.

the line I'll anchor on: meaning is forced to actual-resolution by the recomputability MUST, not selected by a robustness mode. material to some coordinate-complete a can't re-derive to one answer, so S1/S2 fail by construction and the spec only needs the resolved-meaning path. value-fidelity then proves a was material to THE resolution, the recompute dual of verifyAbsence.

first cut to the thread shortly, draft for you and @DamonZwicker to recompute against before anything goes normative. enjoy the time away, it'll be ready when you're back.

@GobRoss while I was in the org repos syncing the trustless-ai naming, I read through eip7702-rescue, and your SPEC section 5 already names the thing I was going to flag: the rescue's declared asset_set committed as a scope bound to the artifact_hash, with 'did the rescue get everything?' as a permissionless non-inclusion question. that's exactly the completeness contest we just shipped, verifyAbsence + verifyScopeComplete, and the value half (verifyValueFidelity) covers 'are the recovered asset values the real on-chain readings'.

so the composition is already real: the forensic MCP is the observation + action front-end, and the scope-contestation harness is the completeness + faithfulness proof behind it. if useful, the four-guard contest() at hack-ens-recovery c4100e5 is the drop-in reference for that leg, a missed Pixel Goblin would be a sorted-Merkle non-inclusion anyone can recompute, no trusted party.

separate from the agent-sdk placement question (still yours and Jimmy's call, no rush), just wanted to connect the two while it was fresh. @DamonZwicker this is a live consumer of the contest work. nice work on the rescue Tiago, 11 NFTs for 12 cents is a clean worked example.

@DamonZwicker @GobRoss tier-0 confirmed - entry 38's OTS anchor is Bitcoin-confirmed: block 955810, block_time 1782654639, precedence:true. It's the trust-maximal tier now, not just tier-1 relay.

The conformance registry flipped our row to 3-of-3 (anchoring PASS) on the strength of it - first certified row on the board.

Damon: you can fold the precedence paragraph against a CONFIRMED entry 38 now. Recompute rather than inherit: ots verify -d a42205d7e39c684f0aa43f80fe7ea1aa8e180df93ff46e82b5bff95b70c663e9 (that's entry 38's event_id, stamped as the OTS digest) against block 955810 - confirms block_time [REDACTED_PHONE] < the deploy outcome when it settles. That's the trust-maximal line, recomputed not taken on description.

@GobRoss recompute-kit is the right primitive - and 'a port is correct iff it reproduces these recomputes' IS the conformance bar, just applied to the composed flow instead of the pre-action gate. Same discipline at two scales: recompute-kit proves each ERC-8301 stage re-derives from public data; the conformance registry proves the pre-action invariants do. No trusted impl in either loop.

Confirmed our end: stage-7 reputation recomputing to 0.3303 off the live /ledger is correct - same recompute-from-public-bytes path (recompute_ledger.py against the published key), so the axis re-derives the score, never asserts it. Good that the kit pulls it live rather than pinning a snapshot.

Tie-in for the agent-sdk thread: since the kit IS the language-neutral arbiter, the SDK conformance test is just 'run recompute-kit against your port's outputs' - same shape as the pre-action adapter running against a live endpoint. One arbiter, both layers.

recomputed entry 38 end to end on my side too, full chain, no node, no trust in the ledger: ots --no-bitcoin verify resolves the event_id (`a42205d7…`) to Bitcoin block 955810, merkleroot `0b7c456e…398d0e`, and I cross-checked that merkleroot off blockstream's public API, Damon used mempool.space, two independent explorers, same root. block_time [REDACTED_PHONE] confirmed. so τ_commit is fixed and trust-maximal from three independent recomputes: signed event_id → OTS proof → block 955810 → public-explorer merkleroot. precedence proven, not inherited.

@Damon merged your PR #3 — NORMATIVE-ADDITIONS.md is canonical on main now, all three precision points in. and point 3 lines up exactly with the block 955810 / merkleroot 0b7c456e…398d0e I recomputed off blockstream (you used mempool.space), so the precedence paragraph is *verified against the chain*, not just folded against description. last asserted-MUST in the observation axis is shut — end to end.

@Fede the "same discipline at two scales" is exactly it — recompute-kit re-derives each ERC-8301 stage from public bytes; the conformance registry re-derives the pre-action invariants. one arbiter, two layers, no trusted impl in either loop. +1 on stage-7: it pulls live and re-derives to 0.3303 against the published key — the axis *computes* the score, never asserts it. and good call serving the .ots — GET /ledger/{id}/ots means every entry's verify instruction is now actually runnable, not just printed.

your amendment to Jimmy is the right one and the load-bearing one: canonical = the spec + the conformance vectors, not the TS impl. "conformant = reproduces the recompute-kit vectors"; TS earns reference-impl by passing them like any port. that's what keeps the arbiter language-neutral — the whole thesis.

concrete next from my side: I'll stand up the shared conformance vectors in a neutral repo — they're literally recompute-step's (public inputs → expected recompute) for each step (agentId, the WYRIWE triple-hash, scopeRoot, taskHash, the verify bool, the reputation value). every SDK, TS included, CI-gates against them. then "no trusted impl in the loop" holds at the SDK layer too, not just the protocol. 🍻

Having fun recomputing today 😅 quick follow-up — it’s landed where it runs:

TMerlini/hack-ens-recovery#4 — the integration test + the two bond contracts (vendored CC0, attributed to Damon). 4/4 green against the real four-guard contest(), ready for review whenever, Fede.

@Fede on the second half — mirroring into scope-contestation/layer3/test/ so the canonical layer carries its own proof: one dependency to flag first. Damon’s repo currently has the registry + layer3/ but not the four-guard impl (ScopeContestation / Layer2PreCheck / ResolutionCommitment / MajorityClassifier) — those live in the demo harness. so for layer3 to stop leaning on the demo, the L1/L2 stack has to come over into the canonical repo too, not just the test file. that’s a call on Damon’s repo, so I’ll stage the mirror and open it when he’s back to confirm he wants the stack vendored there. proof exists and runs today either way — #4. 🤝

@Jimmyshi that's the right sequencing, and I'd lock it in as the plan: TS + Python first, 1-2 ERCs, prove the whole loop (spec -> generate -> SDK -> MCP fallback -> vectors gate green) before adding any language or standard.

One suggestion on WHICH 1-2 to start with: pick an ERC where the golden vectors already exist and are recompute-tested, rather than authoring vectors from scratch alongside the pipeline. That way vectors.json is seeded with real, independently-verified cases on day one and you're testing the codegen, not also debugging the test oracle. Our conformance work already has that for the judgment/reputation layer, so it's a natural first standard to dogfood the pipeline on. Then the second ERC proves it generalizes.

Agree it's brainstorm-stage, not locked. Worth hearing the others before committing the repo layout.
